{
  "$comment": "Backs the week 3 display-name check. It answers one question about a From header: does the display name claim to be a person or an organization whose real domain differs from the sending domain? Authentication cannot answer it, because an attacker who owns a lookalike domain can publish SPF and DKIM for that domain and collect three passes while lying in the display name. Every name here is one a sender may legitimately write, with the domains that own it: the employees and shared mailboxes of the organization, and every vendor, provider, customer, marketer and small business in sender_reputation.json. Attacker brands are deliberately absent, because nothing legitimately owns them; a display name that resolves to no entry claims no known name and the check reports nothing. mismatch_indicator names the indicator code to emit when the sending domain is not an owner, and is null for the classes the week 3 vocabulary has no code for, where the check reports the owner domain and no code. Names whose notes end in decoy never appear in the corpus. unowned_display_names holds the corpus display names that deliberately resolve to no entry, so that \"claims no known name\" is a checked state and not an oversight.",
  "corpus_clock": "2026-04-29T09:00:00-05:00",
  "organization": "pellgrave.example",
  "claims_values": [
    "person",
    "organization"
  ],
  "owner_classes": [
    "organization",
    "vendor",
    "customer",
    "provider",
    "marketer",
    "small_business",
    "individual"
  ],
  "match_rule": "Lowercase the display name, decode any RFC 2047 encoded word, replace every run of characters that is not a letter, a digit or a hyphen with one space, and trim. Do the same to every display_name and alias. Keep the entries whose display_name or alias appears in the normalized display name as a whole run of words, and take the entry whose matched text is longest; ties cannot occur because no two entries share a name. Report that entry by its display_name, never by the alias that matched. With no entry, the check reports nothing. With one entry, compare the From domain, lowercased: it is the owner when it equals a string in owner_domains or ends in a dot followed by one, and the check then reports nothing. Any other From domain means the display name claims a name it does not own, and the check reports the entry's display_name, its claims value and its owner_domains, with the mismatch_indicator code when that field is not null. The suffix test is what a subdomain trick fails: a host that carries an owner name as an inner label rather than as the ending is not the owner.",
